tisan lean under the current map", "Democratic-tilted national environment and market pricing near 9% for Democrats keep this short of Safe"], "rationale": "Van Duyne holds an overwhelming cash-on-hand advantage over Burge, and five of nine expert raters put the seat in the Safe or Solid Republican tier, implying a double-digit Republican margin. The district's R+8 lean and the absence of any outside spending against the incumbent outweigh a favorable national environment for Democrats.", "watch": "A late wave of Democratic outside spending into the district, or new polling showing the national environment pulling suburban Dallas-Fort Worth voters harder against the incumbent, could narrow this toward the Lean R range.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Beth Van Duyne (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Rep. Beth Van Duyne seeks another term in a North Texas district that leans Republican by about eight points. She faces Democrat Kevin Burge, who emerged from a primary runoff but has raised only about $210,000 and had roughly $37,000 in the bank as of late September. Van Duyne, by contrast, has raised about $2.5 million and holds nearly $2.9 million on hand, with no outside groups spending against either candidate.\n\nNo public polls cover the race. Raters overwhelmingly place it in the Safe or Solid Republican column, with several calling it Likely R, and prediction markets give Burge roughly a nine percent chance. A Democratic-leaning national environment offers some hope for an upset, but the money gap and the district's lean make Van Duyne a clear favorite to hold the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0657, "margin": -9.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0832, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0803, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}]}